StillGood is a food waste app. You snap a photo of your groceries, it reminds you before they go off, and it suggests recipes to use them up. I designed and built all of it on my own: the phone app, the system behind it and the website.

I did that with Claude Code, an AI coding assistant, working next to me. This post is about how that worked in practice.

## Me deciding, the AI typing

The split was simple. I decided how the app should work: the screens, what each one does, how data moves between the app and the server. Claude Code wrote a lot of the code to make that happen, much faster than I could type it.

What made it work was giving it clear, written rules to follow, and treating every change like one from a new teammate. Each change went through a pull request, and I read and tested it before it went in.

## What I built with

The app is React Native with Expo, so it runs on Android from one codebase. Behind it sit a Fastify server and a Supabase database. Google's Gemini AI reads grocery photos and suggests recipes, and PostHog shows me how people actually use the app.

## What I'd tell someone starting out

An AI assistant makes you faster, not finished. The idea, the design, the testing and the launch were still mine, and so was every mistake that I caught before users did.

StillGood went live on the Google Play Store on 26 August 2026.
